--TEST--
Core\Csv::format's quoting set, swept over every 7-bit byte as a one-field record, is exactly the separator, the quote, CR and LF — where fputcsv's is those four plus the space and the tab
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
The neighbouring `csv-format-writes-the-records-fputcsv-writes` pins the two writers byte for byte over the four bytes that change a parse, and the sweep below is what says there is no fifth: the two bands differ in exactly two columns, `0x09` and `0x20`, where `fputcsv` quotes and this does not. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/csv.rs`'s *Writing: one rule, four bytes* section is the home of that decision — neither byte changes a parse, RFC 4180 § 2.4 keeps the spaces inside a field either way, and quoting on them makes a diff of two exports noisy for no reader's benefit — so this case exists to measure the set rather than to re-argue it. Nothing is lost by the narrower rule, which is the last two lines: the space field is written bare, read back with its space, and a PHP reader is no worse off, because `str_getcsv` keeps an unquoted field's spaces exactly as it keeps a quoted one's. The divergence runs one way only — there is no byte this quotes and `fputcsv` writes bare — so a document written here is one PHP reads, and the reverse direction is the reader's, pinned in `csv-parse-reads-one-record-exactly-as-str_getcsv-does`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A field is quoted or it is not, so the question each byte asks is whether
// the record it is written into begins with the quote character. Only a sweep
// answers whether the set is *exactly* the four the module doc names, rather
// than those four plus one nobody wrote a row for.
final class Show {
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }

    public static function of(string $document): string {
        return "[" . Core\Str::replaceAll(
            $document,
            ["\r" => "<CR>", "\n" => "<LF>", "\t" => "<TAB>"],
        ) . "]";
    }
}

// The band is the whole answer and the list beneath it is the same answer
// spelled out, because a band of 128 characters is read by counting and a
// miscount is exactly what this case is here to prevent.
string $band = "";
string $list = "";
for (int $b = 0; $b < 128; $b = $b + 1) {
    array<array<string>> $rows = [[Core\Str::fromCodePoint($b as uint)]];
    bool $quoted = Core\Str::startsWith(Core\Csv::format($rows), "\"");
    $band = $band . Show::yn($quoted);
    if ($quoted) {
        $list = $list . " " . $b;
    }
}
echo $band, "\n";
echo "quoted:", $list, "\n";

// The consequence, in one record: the two bytes PHP quotes for are written
// bare here, and the two that change a parse are quoted on both sides.
array<array<string>> $mixed = [["a b", "c\td", "e,f"]];
string $document = Core\Csv::format($mixed);
echo Show::of($document), "\n";

// And nothing is lost by writing them bare — the reader hands back the field
// it was given, spaces and tab included.
foreach (Core\Csv::parse($document) as array<string> $record) {
    foreach ($record as string $field) {
        echo "<", Core\Str::replaceAll($field, ["\t" => "<TAB>"]), ">";
    }
}
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
nnnnnnnnnnynnynnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnynnnnnnnnnynnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnnn
quoted: 10 13 34 44
[a b,c<TAB>d,"e,f"<LF>]
<a b><c<TAB>d><e,f>
